{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"photo-client-session-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Photo client session track","description":"Takes one paid portrait, family or small event session from client brief to delivered gallery in gated steps (brief, plan, shoot day, cull and edit, delivery and backup).","category":"photography","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","ship"],"role":["artist","consultant"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["message","notes","preferences"],"output":["plan","checklist","message"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["client-photography","portrait-session","photo-delivery","shoot-planning","photo-backup"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-session-prep-guide","plan-portrait-shoot","cull-photo-shoot","match-edits-across-photo-set"],"personas":["photography-mentor"]},"args":[{"name":"client_request","description":"What the client asked for (paste their message if you have it), the session type, date, location ideas and any deadline.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"your_setup","description":"Your gear, editing app, experience, and the terms you already have (session length, number of images, delivery time). Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/01-brief.md"},{"id":"plan","file":"steps/02-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/02-shoot-plan.md"},{"id":"shoot-day","file":"steps/03-shoot-day.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/03-run-sheet.md"},{"id":"cull-edit","file":"steps/04-cull-edit.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/04-cull-and-edit.md"},{"id":"delivery","file":"steps/05-delivery.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"client-session/05-delivery.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs one client photo session the way an experienced portrait photographer does: agree what the client needs before planning, plan light and shots before the day, protect the files from the first minute, and deliver what was promised on time. Each step builds on the one before and stops for the photographer's approval.\n\n<client_request>\n{{client_request}}\n</client_request>\n{{#your_setup}}\n<your_setup>\n{{your_setup}}\n</your_setup>\n{{/your_setup}}\n\nRules for every step: the photographer decides prices and policies, so never invent fees, image counts or delivery times; use theirs or mark a placeholder. Keep the client's must-haves from step 1 visible in every later step. Do not state laws or contract terms as fact; suggest checking local rules and, for contracts, a professional. If the photographer wants to skip to the shoot day, explain in one line why the brief comes first, offer to run step 1 quickly, and keep every gate.","steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/01-brief.md","text":"# Step 1: Brief\n\nPin down what the client needs before planning anything.\n\n1. From the request, write what is known: session type, people, date, location ideas, use of the photos (wall prints, cards, website), deadline.\n2. List what is missing and write a short, friendly reply to the client asking only those questions (who will be there, must-have shots, style they like, any mobility, sensory or cultural needs, consent for sharing images of children or on the photographer's portfolio).\n3. Note the terms that must be agreed in writing before the date (session length, images included, delivery time, rescheduling, deposit, usage), using the photographer's own terms or placeholders.\n4. Flag risks: tight deadline, large group, midday-only availability, indoor venue with poor light.\n\nWrite the document with sections Known, Questions for the client, Terms to confirm, Risks.\n\nStop and wait for approval before planning the shoot."},{"id":"plan","file":"steps/02-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/02-shoot-plan.md","text":"# Step 2: Shoot plan\n\nPlan light, places and shots for the approved brief.\n\n1. Timing: the time of day that gives the best light for the location and season, with a weather backup and the rescheduling rule from step 1.\n2. Locations: two or three spots within the location with what each offers (open shade, backlight, clean background), and a walking order.\n3. Shot list: must-haves from step 1 first, then groupings (everyone, pairs, individuals, details), with a target number of frames per grouping.\n4. Gear and settings: what to pack (with spares: batteries, cards), starting settings per spot, and a light modifier or reflector if useful.\n5. Client prep: point to a prep guide (what to wear, what to bring) and draft the reminder message for two days before.\n\nWrite the document with sections Timing, Locations, Shot list, Gear and settings, Client prep.\n\nStop and wait for approval before writing the run sheet."},{"id":"shoot-day","file":"steps/03-shoot-day.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/03-run-sheet.md","text":"# Step 3: Shoot day run sheet\n\nTurn the plan into a timed sheet for the day.\n\n1. A timeline from arrival to wrap in minutes, with buffers, breaks for children or older people, and the must-haves early while energy is high.\n2. The first five minutes: greeting, setting expectations, a warm-up prompt.\n3. Data safety: dual card slots or swapping cards mid-session, and copying cards to two places the same day before formatting anything.\n4. If things go wrong: late arrival, rain, a tired child, a camera fault, and what to drop from the shot list first.\n\nWrite the document with sections Timeline, First five minutes, Data safety, If things go wrong.\n\nStop and wait for approval, then shoot. Come back after the session to cull and edit."},{"id":"cull-edit","file":"steps/04-cull-edit.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"client-session/04-cull-and-edit.md","text":"# Step 4: Cull and edit\n\nGet from the card to a finished set.\n\n1. Ask how many frames were shot and whether the backup from step 3 is done; if not, do that first.\n2. Cull in passes (rejects, picks, final) to the number of images promised, checking every must-have from step 1 is covered; list any that were missed and how to tell the client.\n3. Edit: one look for the session, corrections per lighting scene before the shared look, natural skin tones, and light retouching only (no reshaping unless the client asked and agreed).\n4. A grid check for consistency before export.\n\nWrite the document with sections Cull, Must-have coverage, Edit, Consistency check.\n\nStop and wait for approval before delivery."},{"id":"delivery","file":"steps/05-delivery.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"client-session/05-delivery.md","text":"# Step 5: Delivery and backup\n\nHand over and close the session.\n\n1. Export settings for each use from step 1 (full resolution for prints, web size for sharing) and file naming.\n2. A delivery message to the client: how to download, how long the link lasts (the photographer's own policy or a placeholder), print advice, and a gentle request for a review.\n3. Archive: where the finals and originals are kept, how many copies, one offsite, and how long they are retained (the photographer's policy).\n4. Portfolio use: only with the consent recorded in step 1.\n5. Notes for next time: what worked and what to change.\n\nWrite the document with sections Export, Delivery message, Archive, Portfolio use, Notes for next time."}]}
